  "slug": "segmentation-topic-6c02",
  "summary": "Recent studies show weak added modalities can degrade recurrence-free survival accuracy, making input composition a central determinant of model performance.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how inputs are structured, selected and combined before learning. In prostate cancer, adding weak histology, radiology and clinical modalities degrades biochemical recurrence-free survival accuracy, so additional inputs are not uniformly beneficial. Copula fusion of clinical and gene-expression risk scores yields no AUC gain over the clinical model for breast cancer mortality, reinforcing this caution. On the whole-slide side, TDAM-CRC links MIL features from colorectal cancer images to the multi-omics hub gene MRPL37. Single-cell items address related questions: a ZINB-based denoiser with InfoVAE-GAN latent factors, a fusion of four feature types across six strategies for cell typing, and a benchmark of 16 deep learning integration methods. What is new is explicit evaluation of input composition and fusion strategy against baseline models, rather than architecture alone.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-20"
}
